\section{What counts as reviewable, and what is excluded}{

Two populations are removed before the queue is built, following
\code{dev/ADJUDICATION-PLAN.md} section 4, and both are \strong{reported rather than
dropped} — their dollars are in the returned counts, because a queue that
silently excludes a population looks identical to one with nothing to exclude:
\itemize{
\item \strong{non-itemizing returns} — a recipient name that is a pointer
(\verb{SEE ATTACHED} and its variants, \code{\link[=pf_placeholder_pattern]{pf_placeholder_pattern()}}). No review can
resolve a pointer; these need the attachment, see \code{\link[=pf_attached_report]{pf_attached_report()}}.
\item \strong{foreign recipients} — no BMF row exists to find, so review cannot
succeed. Detected from \code{oos_reason}.
}
}

\section{Why \code{grants} is usually required}{

\code{\link[=pf_recipients]{pf_recipients()}} keeps one representative row per identity and carries no
dollar total, so the amount on a matched identity is one member's amount
rather than the cluster's. Pass the every-row frame from \code{\link[=pf_prepare]{pf_prepare()}}
(\code{p$grants}, which carries \code{recipient_uid}) and the dollars are summed per
identity. Where the match was \emph{not} built from deduped identities, each row is
its own identity and the match alone is sufficient — that case is detected,
not guessed.
}

